}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"All sentences successfully stored in ChromaDB!\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":16},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Retrieve Embeddings\n\nNow let's retrieve the stored vectors.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Connect to database\nclient = chromadb.PersistentClient(\n    path=\"/kaggle/working/chroma_db\"\n)\n\n# Get collection\ncollection = client.get_collection(\n    name=\"sentences\"\n)\n\n# Fetch all records\nresults = collection.get(\n    include=[\"documents\", \"embeddings\", \"metadatas\"]\n)\n\nprint(f\"{'ID':<38} | {'Sentence':<35} | {'Embedding':<45} | {'Metadata'}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 150)\n\nfor i in range(len(results[\"ids\"])):\n\n    record_id = results[\"ids\"][i]\n    sentence = results[\"documents\"][i]\n    embedding = results[\"embeddings\"][i]\n    metadata = results[\"metadatas\"][i]\n\n    # Display first and last 5 dimensions\n    embedding_display = f\"{embedding[:5]} ... {embedding[-5:]}\"\n\n    print(\n        f\"{record_id:<10} | \"\n        f\"{sentence:<35} | \"\n        f\"{str(embedding_display):<45} | \"\n        f\"{metadata}\"\n    )","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.6248Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.625441Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.674903Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.6254Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.673409Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"ID                                     | Sentence                            | Embedding                                     | Metadata\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n0          | I love machine learning             | [-0.04363631 -0.0590543   0.08201234 -0.01076717  0.0611959 ] ... [ 0.10032279  0.06195568  0.09771024  0.01688083 -0.09040587] | {'page': 1, 'category': 'AI', 'source': 'document.pdf'}\n1          | I enjoy coding                      | [-0.05627798 -0.03124129 -0.03912254  0.01142177  0.03994456] ... [ 0.10028355  0.02995748  0.08396302  0.00678    -0.05171905] | {'source': 'document.pdf', 'category': 'AI', 'page': 2}\n2          | I like eating pizza                 | [-0.06539407  0.01154024  0.00051851  0.10881377 -0.09285428] ... [ 0.04528106  0.06465604  0.09007934  0.02154538 -0.08354521] | {'page': 3, 'source': 'document.pdf', 'category': 'AI'}\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":17},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Production Database\n\nSQLite is fine for learning, but production semantic search systems commonly use vector databases.\n\n| Database              | Purpose                                |\n| --------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| SQLite                | Basic embedding storage                |\n| PostgreSQL + pgvector | Relational database with vector search |\n| FAISS                 | Fast similarity search                 |\n| ChromaDB              | Developer-friendly vector database     |\n| Pinecone              | Managed vector database                |\n\n\n## Popular vector databases\n\nThere are several vector database technologies. You should understand their differences before choosing one for a project.\n\n### 1. Pinecone\n\nA managed cloud vector database. You don't need to manage the underlying database infrastructure yourself.\n\n- Managed infrastructure.\n- Similarity search.\n- Metadata filtering.\n- Production RAG applications.\n\nBest suited to: Teams wanting managed vector infrastructure.\n\n[Official website](https://www.pinecone.io/)\n\n### 2. Qdrant\n\nAn open-source vector database with cloud-hosted options.\n\n- HNSW-based vector search.\n- Payload/metadata filtering.\n- Quantization.\n- Dense and sparse vector support.\n\nBest suited to: Production semantic search and RAG.\n\n[Official website](https://qdrant.tech/)\n\n### 3. Milvus\n\nA vector database designed for large-scale vector search.\n\n- Multiple indexing algorithms.\n- Distributed architecture.\n- High-dimensional vector search.\n- Large-scale workloads.\n\nBest suited to: Large-scale vector retrieval.\n\n[Official website](https://milvus.io/)\n\n### 4. Weaviate\n\nAn open-source vector database with integrated AI-oriented capabilities.\n\n- Semantic search.\n- Hybrid search.\n- Metadata filtering.\n- Vectorization integrations.\n\nBest suited to: AI applications requiring flexible search.\n\n[Official website](https://weaviate.io/)\n\n### 5. Chroma\n\nA developer-friendly vector database often used for prototyping and RAG projects.\n\n- Simple Python API.\n- Local persistence.\n- Metadata filtering.\n- Embedding integrations.\n\nBest suited to: Learning, prototyping, and smaller applications.\n\n[Official website](https://www.trychroma.com/)\n\n### 6. FAISS\n\nA vector similarity search library developed by Meta AI.\n\n- Flat search.\n- IVF.\n- PQ.\n- HNSW-related indexes.\n- CPU and GPU capabilities for supported indexes.\n\nBest suited to: Building custom similarity search systems and experimenting with indexing.\n\n[Official documentation](https://faiss.ai/)\n\n# Architecture of Vector Database\n\nThis is one of the most important concepts for AI engineering interviews.\n\nThere are two main processes:\n\n- Ingestion: inserting and indexing vectors.\n- Retrieval: searching for similar vectors.\n\n![Vector Database Architecture](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/vector-database-architecture.png)\n\n## Ingestion process\n\nSuppose you have 100,000 PDF documents.\n\nYou want to make them searchable using semantic search.\n\nThe workflow is:\n\n1. Collect documents\n\n    - PDF files\n    - Word documents\n    - Text files\n    - HTML pages\n\n2. Extract text: Extract the text from the documents.\n\n3. Chunk the text: Large documents are divided into smaller chunks.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nDocument:\n\"Machine learning is a field of AI.\nIt allows computers to learn from data.\nDeep learning uses neural networks...\"\n```\n\nAfter chunking:\n\n```\nChunk 1:\n\"Machine learning is a field of AI.\"\n\nChunk 2:\n\"It allows computers to learn from data.\"\n\nChunk 3:\n\"Deep learning uses neural networks.\"\n```\n\n4. Generate embeddings: Send each chunk to an embedding model.\n\n5. Store the embeddings: Store the generated vectors, IDs, text, and metadata in the vector database.\n\n6. Build an index: The database creates an index that helps find similar vectors without comparing every vector individually.\n\nWe'll discuss indexing algorithms in detail below.\n\n## Retrieval process\n\nNow suppose the user asks:\n\n> How does machine learning work?\n\nThe workflow is:\n\n1. Receive the user's query.\n2. Convert the query into an embedding using the same compatible embedding model.\n3. Search the vector index.\n4. Calculate similarity between the query vector and stored vectors.\n5. Retrieve the nearest matching records.\n6. Return the corresponding text and metadata.\n\nNotice something important:\n\n> The vector database generally does not generate embeddings itself.\n\n> An external embedding model usually converts the text into vectors. Some vector database products also provide integrated embedding or inference functionality, but that is a separate capability.\n\n# Search Techniques\nSearch techniques are methods used to retrieve relevant documents or information from a collection based on a user's query. In vector databases and information retrieval systems, search techniques help identify the most relevant documents efficiently.\n\nThere are two major approaches to searching:\n\n1. Keyword Search: Finds documents based on exact words or terms.\n2. Semantic Search: Finds documents based on the meaning and context of the query.\n\nA combination of keyword search and semantic search is often used to improve search accuracy. This approach is known as Hybrid Search\n\n![Search Technique](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/search-techniques.png)\n\n## Keyword Search vs Semantic Search\n\n| Feature | Keyword Search | Semantic Search |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Main approach | Term matching | Meaning matching |\n| Data structure | Inverted index / sparse index | Dense vector index |\n| Techniques | BM25, TF IDF | Embeddings |\n| Exact matching | Excellent | Not guaranteed |\n| Synonym understanding | Limited by analyzer | Usually better |\n| Misspellings | Depends on configuration | Sometimes more tolerant |\n| Technical identifiers | Excellent | Can be unreliable |\n| Computational requirements | Often relatively inexpensive | Requires embedding and vector search |\n\nSuppose the user searches:\n\n\"car\"\n\nKeyword search may return:\n\n- Car maintenance.\n- Car insurance.\n- Car engines.\n\nSemantic search may also return:\n\n- Automobile maintenance.\n- Vehicle insurance.\n- Electric vehicles.\n\nKeyword search focuses on matching terms, whereas semantic search focuses on conceptual similarity.\n\n# Keyword Search\n\nKeyword Search is a traditional information retrieval technique used to find documents based on the occurrence of specific words or terms in a user's query.\n\nFrom a vector database perspective, keyword search is important because not every search problem is semantic. Sometimes users need exact matches rather than conceptually similar results.\n\nFor example, suppose a database contains these documents:\n\n1. Introduction to Machine Learning\n2. Deep Learning with Python\n3. Machine Learning Algorithms\n4. Introduction to Natural Language Processing\n\nIf a user searches for `\"Machine Learning\"`, keyword search identifies documents containing those exact terms rather than documents that are merely semantically related.\n\n**How Keyword Search Works**\n\nKeyword search generally follows these steps:\n\n1. User Query: `\"Machine Learning Algorithms\"`\n2. Text Processing: Tokenization, normalization, stop-word removal\n3. Inverted Index Lookup: Find documents containing query terms\n4. Relevance Scoring: Calculate BM25 or TF IDF scores\n5. Ranked Search Results: Return the most relevant documents\n\nVector databases often combine keyword search with vector similarity search to provide more accurate retrieval.\n\n**Popular Vector Databases Supporting Keyword or Sparse Search**\n\n| Vector database | Keyword / sparse retrieval |\n| --- | --- |\n| Elasticsearch | BM25 and inverted index |\n| OpenSearch | BM25 and sparse retrieval |\n| Weaviate | BM25 keyword search |\n| Qdrant | Sparse vectors |\n| Milvus | Sparse vectors and full-text search |\n| Pinecone | Sparse-dense hybrid search |\n\nFrom a vector database perspective, keyword search is not necessarily an alternative to vector search. It is another retrieval mechanism that complements semantic search.\n\n- TF-IDF: Measures the importance of words in documents.\n- BM25: Improves keyword ranking using term frequency saturation and document length normalization.\n- Inverted Index: Efficiently finds documents containing specific terms.\n- Sparse Vectors: Represent keyword-based information in a vector-like structure.\n- Dense Vectors: Represent semantic meaning using embeddings.\n\nThe combination of keyword search and semantic search is called Hybrid Search. It helps retrieve documents that are both textually relevant and semantically relevant.\n\n## TF IDF\n\nTF IDF is a statistical technique used to determine how important a word is in a document relative to an entire collection of documents.\n\nThe basic idea:\n\n- A word appearing frequently in a particular document is potentially important to that document.\n- A word appearing in almost every document is less useful for distinguishing documents.\n\nTF IDF has two components.\n\n### Term Frequency (TF)\n\nTerm Frequency measures how frequently a term appears in a document.\n\nA simple formula:\n\n$$\nTF(t,d)=\\frac{f_{t,d}}{|d|}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $t$ = term.\n- $d$ = document.\n- $f_{t,d}$ = frequency of term in document.\n- $|d|$ = total number of terms in the document.\n\nFor example:\n\nDocument: \"Vector database is a database technology.\"\n\nThe word \"database\" appears twice in a document containing seven words.\n\n$$\nTF(database,d)=\\frac{2}{7}\n$$\n\n### Inverse Document Frequency (IDF)\n\nIDF measures how rare or distinctive a term is across the document collection.\n\n$$\nIDF(t)=\\log\\left(\\frac{N}{DF(t)}\\right)\n$$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $N$ = total number of documents.\n- $DF(t)$ = number of documents containing the term.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Total documents = 1000.\n- Documents containing \"database\" = 100.\n\n$$\nIDF(database)=\\log(1000/100)\n$$\n\nA rare term gets a higher IDF score, while a common term gets a lower score.\n\n### Final TF IDF Score\n\n$$\nTFIDF(t,d)=TF(t,d)\\times IDF(t)\n$$\n\nA document receives a higher score for terms that occur frequently in that document but are relatively rare across the collection.\n\nIn vector databases: TF IDF can be used as a sparse representation of documents, where each dimension represents a vocabulary term and its value represents its importance.\n\nHowever, TF IDF is not itself a vector similarity metric. The resulting sparse vectors can subsequently be compared using cosine similarity or other scoring methods.\n\nA **higher TF-IDF score** generally indicates that a term is **more important** in a document relative to the collection.\n\n**Advantages of TF-IDF:**\n\n- Simple and easy to implement.\n- Helps identify important keywords.\n- Reduces the importance of common words.\n- Useful for document retrieval and text classification.\n\n**Limitations of TF-IDF:**\n\n- Does not understand the meaning of words.\n- Does not understand synonyms.\n- Does not consider word context.\n- May give similar words different scores even when their meanings are related.\n\n### How the inverted index works\n\nThe most important data structure behind traditional keyword search is the Inverted Index.\n\nAn inverted index maps each word to the documents in which that word appears.\n\nConsider these documents:\n\n| Document | Content |\n| --- | --- |\n| D1 | Vector database search |\n| D2 | Machine learning search |\n| D3 | Vector database indexing |\n| D4 | Machine learning algorithms |\n\nThe inverted index looks like this:\n\n| Term | Document IDs |\n| --- | --- |\n| Vector | D1, D3 |\n| Database | D1, D3 |\n| Search | D1, D2 |\n| Machine | D2, D4 |\n| Learning | D2, D4 |\n| Indexing | D3 |\n| Algorithms | D4 |\n\n**Example query: `\"Vector Database\"`**\n\n- Vector: D1, D3\n- Database: D1, D3\n\nCombine matching document IDs\n\nMatching Documents: D1, D3\n\nInstead of scanning every document to find a word, the search engine looks up that word in the inverted index.\n\nThis significantly improves search performance for large datasets.\n\nIn practice, inverted indexes may also store:\n\n- Term frequency.\n- Document frequency.\n- Term positions.\n- Field information.\n- Document lengths.\n\nThese additional details help calculate relevance scores and support phrase searches.\n\n[Learn More](https://www.kaggle.com/code/masum1834e/ann-classification-on-uci-spambase-data#TF-IDF-Vectorizer)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n\nvectorizer = TfidfVectorizer()\ntfidf_matrix = vectorizer.fit_transform(sentences1)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.676255Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.676906Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.761149Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.676839Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.759978Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":18},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(vectorizer.get_feature_names_out())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:32.762444Z","iopu