t","data":{"text/plain":"array([0, 2, 1])"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":31},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Step 6: Display results\nfor index in ranked_indices:\n    print(\n        sentences1[index],\n        \"Score:\",\n        scores[index]\n    )","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.699315Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.699689Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.727467Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.699648Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.726393Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"I love machine learning Score: 0.9814990150962266\nI like eating pizza Score: 0.0\nI enjoy coding Score: 0.0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":32},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## TF IDF vs BM25\n\n| Feature | TF IDF | BM25 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Type | Keyword ranking | Keyword ranking |\n| Term frequency | Yes | Yes |\n| Inverse document frequency | Yes | Yes |\n| Document length normalization | Not inherent | Yes |\n| Frequency saturation | No | Yes |\n| Complexity | Simple | More advanced |\n| Ranking quality | Good baseline | Often better |\n\nRemember:\n\n- TF IDF = How important is a word in a document?\n- BM25 = How relevant is a document to a query using BM25 scoring?\n- Cosine Similarity = How similar are two document/query vectors?\n\nThis distinction is fundamental to understanding how keyword search and semantic search work.\n\n# Semantic Searching\n\nThis is probably the most important mathematical concept behind vector databases.\n\nWhen you search for something, the database needs to determine which stored vectors are closest to your query vector.\n\nThere are several similarity and distance metrics.\n\nThe three most important are:\n\n1. Cosine similarity\n2. Euclidean distance (L2)\n3. Dot product (inner product)\n\nLet's understand each one.\n\n![Semantic Searching](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/semantic_searching.png)\n\n## Cosine similarity\n\nCosine similarity measures the angle between two vectors.\n\nIt tells us how similar the directions of two vectors are.\n\nThe formula is:\n\n$$ \\text{Cosine Similarity}(A,B)= \\frac{A\\cdot B}{||A||\\\\.||B||} $$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $A$ = Query vector.\n- $B$ = Stored vector.\n- $A\\cdot B$ = Dot product.\n- $||A||$ = Magnitude (length) of vector A.\n- $||B||$ = Magnitude of vector B.\n\nLet's use a simple example.\n\nSuppose:\n\n$$ A=[1,2] $$\n\n$$ B=[2,4] $$\n\nFirst, calculate the dot product:\n\n$$ A\\cdot B=(1\\times2)+(2\\times4) $$\n\n$$ =2+8=10 $$\n\nCalculate the magnitude of A:\n\n$$ ||A||=\\sqrt{1^2+2^2} =\\sqrt{5} $$\n\nCalculate the magnitude of B:\n\n$$ ||B||=\\sqrt{2^2+4^2} =\\sqrt{20} $$\n\nNow:\n\n$$ \\text{Similarity}= \\frac{10}{\\sqrt5\\times\\sqrt{20}} $$\n\n$$ =\\frac{10}{10}=1 $$\n\nSimilarity = 1.0\n\nThis means the vectors have exactly the same direction.\n\n### Understanding cosine similarity values\n\n| Score | General interpretation     |\n| ----- | -------------------------- |\n| 1     | Same direction             |\n| 0.9   | Very similar direction     |\n| 0.7   | Somewhat similar direction |\n| 0     | Perpendicular directions   |\n| -1    | Opposite directions        |\n\nThese are geometric interpretations, not universal semantic similarity thresholds. The meaning of a score depends on the embedding model and dataset.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"A = embeddings2[0]\nB = embeddings2[1]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.728611Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.72896Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.75221Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.728918Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.751015Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":33},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(A[:5], A[-5:])\nprint(B[:5], B[-5:])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11