I love machine learning Score: 0.6937381\nI enjoy coding Score: 0.14311592\nI like eating pizza Score: 0.05476463\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":40},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n**When is cosine similarity useful?**\n\n- Semantic search.\n- Document retrieval.\n- RAG applications.\n- Text similarity.\n- Recommendation systems.\n\nIt is particularly useful when vector direction matters more than vector magnitude.\n\n## Euclidean distance (L2 distance)\n\nEuclidean distance measures the straight line distance between two points in vector space.\n\nFormula:\n\n$$\nd(A,B)=\\sqrt{\\sum{i=1}^{n}(A_i-B_i)^2}\n$$\n\nExample:\n\n$$\nA=[1,2]\n$$\n\n$$\nB=[4,6]\n$$\n\nCalculate:\n\n$$\nd(A,B)=\\sqrt{(1-4)^2+(2-6)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\n=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\n=\\sqrt{9+16}\n$$\n\n$$\n=\\sqrt{25}=5\n$$\n\n**Distance = 5**\n\nUnlike cosine similarity:\n\n- Smaller distance means more similar.\n- Larger distance means less similar.\n","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.692367Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.692669Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.700555Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.692644Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.699641Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"distance = np.linalg.norm(A - B)\n\nprint(\"Euclidean Distance:\", distance)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.936318Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.936804Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.960751Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.936759Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.959452Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Euclidean Distance: 0.7404513\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":41},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Dot Product\n\nDot product considers both direction and magnitude.\n\nIt is often used when the embedding model and retrieval setup are designed for inner-product search.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"dot_product = np.dot(A, B)\n\nprint(\"Dot Product:\", dot_product)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.962113Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.962635Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.982702Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.962573Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.981852Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Dot Product: 0.7258659\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":42},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Comparison of similarity algorithms\n\n| Metric             | What it measures        | Best score |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------- | ---------- |\n| Cosine similarity  | Direction similarity    | Higher     |\n| Euclidean distance | Straight line distance  | Lower      |\n| Dot product        | Direction and magnitude | Higher     |\n\nImportant production consideration:\n\nYou should choose the metric according to the embedding model's training and retrieval recommendations.\n\nFor normalized vectors, cosine similarity and dot product produce equivalent rankings because the vector magnitudes are 1.\n\nLikewise, for unitnormalized vectors:\n\n$$\n||A-B||^2=2-2(A \\cdot B)\n$$\n\nThis means maximizing cosine similarity, maximizing dot product, and minimizing squared Euclidean distance give the same ranking.\n\n![Similarity Algorithm](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/cosine_similarity_and_dot_product.png)\n\n## Differences Between Embedding Models\n\nIf TF-IDF and embedding models both convert text into vectors.\n\nThe most important difference is:\n\n- TF-IDF vectorization represents the importance of words in a document.\n- Embedding vectorization represents the semantic meaning of a piece of text.\n\nBoth convert text into numerical vectors, and both can use cosine similarity. However, the vectors represent completely different types of information.\n\nLet's understand this deeply with practical examples.\n\n### TF-IDF Vectorization (Keyword Search)\n\nIt is a statistical technique that converts text into numerical vectors based on the words that appear in the text and how informative those words are across a collection of documents.\n\n**Example**\n\nSuppose we have three documents:\n\n- Document 1: I love machine learning\n- Document 2: I love deep learning\n- Document 3: I love artificial intelligence\n\nFirst, we build a vocabulary from the corpus:\n\nVocabulary\n\n`[I, love, machine, learning, deep, artificial, intelligence]`\n\nTF-IDF assigns a numerical value to each vocabulary term for each document.\n\nTF-IDF values in Document 1\n\n$$[0.1, 0.1, 0.7, 0.5, 0, 0, 0]$$\n\nWhat does this vector mean?\n\n- `machine` has a high weight.\n- `learning` has a moderate weight.\n- `deep`, `artificial`, and `intelligence` have zero weight in this document.\n- The vector is based on the vocabulary and the statistical properties of the corpus.\n\nImportant: TF-IDF does not understand what machine learning means. It only calculates the statistical importance of the terms.\n\n### Embedding Vectorization (Semantic Search)\n\nAn embedding model is a neural network trained to transform text into a numerical vector that captures semantic information.\n\nExamples of embedding models:\n\n- `sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2`\n- `text-embedding-3-small` (OpenAI)\n- `text-embedding-3-large` (OpenAI)\n- `BGE`\n- `E5`\n\nUnlike TF-IDF, embeddings do not simply assign weights to vocabulary words.\n\nThey learn a representation in which texts with similar meanings tend to have vectors that are close to one another.\n\n**Example**\n\nSuppose we have a documents. We pass each document through an embedding model.\n\nAn embedding vector might look conceptually like this:\n\n```\nDocument 1:[0.021, -0.134, 0.087, 0.452, ..., -0.091]\n```\n\nNotice something important.\n\nThese numbers do not directly represent the weights of the words `I`, `love`, `machine`, or `learning`.\n\nInstead, the dimensions collectively encode learned semantic features. Individual dimensions are usually not directly interpretable as specific words or concepts.\n\nFor example, the embedding model may represent these sentences as semantically similar:\n\n- \"I love machine learning.\"\n- \"I enjoy studying artificial intelligence.\"\n\nEven though many of their words are different.\n\n### The Fundamental Differences\n\nLet's compare them directly.\n\n| Property               | TF-IDF Vector                          | Embedding Vector                                         |\n| ---------------------- | -------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Technique              | Statistical                            | Neural network                                           |\n| Purpose                | Represent word importance              | Represent semantic meaning                               |\n| Word importance        | Explicitly calculated                  | Learned indirectly                                       |\n| Vocabulary             | Requires a corpus vocabulary           | Uses a trained model's tokenizer                         |\n| Vector dimensions      | Usually vocabulary size                | Fixed model dimension                                    |\n| Vector type            | Usually sparse                         | Usually dense                                            |\n| Semantic understanding | No inherent understanding              | Learns semantic relationships                            |\n| Synonyms               | Usually not recognized as equivalent   | Often represented as similar                             |\n| Context                | Limited                                | Can capture context, depending on model                  |\n| New words              | Requires vocabulary handling           | Model tokenizer can handle many unseen words as subwords |\n| Training               | No neural training required for TF-IDF | Model is pretrained or trained                           |\n| Storage                | Sparse vectors are efficient           | Dense vectors typically need more storage                |\n\nLet's focus on three particularly important differences.\n\n#### Sparse vector vs. dense vector\n\n**TF-IDF**\n\n- Sparse vector: most dimensions may be zero.\n\nFor TF-IDF:\n\n```\n[0, 0, 0.8, 0, 0.4, 0, 0, 0]\n```\n\n**Embedding**\n\n- Dense vector: most dimensions contain values.\n\nFor an embedding:\n\n```\n[0.12, -0.31, 0.08, 0.44, -0.21, 0.19, ...]\n```\n\nTF-IDF vector dimensions correspond to terms in the vocabulary.\n\nEmbedding dimensions correspond to learned numerical features.\n\n#### Exact word matching vs. semantic matching\n\nSuppose your database contains:\n\n```\nDocument A: I drive a car.\nDocument B: I drive an automobile.\nDocument C: I enjoy eating pizza.\n```\n\nYour search query is:\n\n```\nI drive a car.\n```\n\n##### TF-IDF keyword search\n\n**Document A**\n\n- Strong match\n- Exact terms\n\n**Document B**\n\n- May score lower\n- Different word\n\n**Document C**\n\n- Low match\n- Different meaning\n\nTF-IDF mainly relies on shared vocabulary. Because `car` and `automobile` are different terms, TF-IDF doesn't inherently know they mean the same thing.\n\n##### Embedding semantic search\n\n**Document A**\n\n- Very similar\n- Same words + meaning\n\n**Document B**\n\n- Likely similar\n- Similar meaning\n\n**Document C**\n\n- Less similar\n- Different meaning\n\nAn embedding model trained to capture semantic similarity may recognize that `car` and `automobile` are related.\n\nThis is why semantic search can retrieve relevant documents even when the query and document use different words.\n\n#### What does cosine similarity actually measure?\n\n\nThe difference is the meaning of the input vectors.\n\nCosine similarity measures the angle between two vectors:\n\nBut what are those vectors representing?\n\n**TF-IDF + Cosine**\n\n- Measures similarity in word-weight space\n- How much do these documents share important terms?\n\n**Embedding + Cosine**\n\n- Measures similarity in semantic space\n- How similar are the meanings of these texts according to the embedding model?\n\nThe cosine similarity formula is the same. The vectorization technique is different.\n\n# Metadata Filtering\n\nMetadata filtering lets you restrict vector search to records that satisfy particular conditions.\n\nImagine you have 1 million documents from different departments:\n\n```\n{\n    \"text\": \"Employee salary information...\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n        \"department\": \"HR\",\n        \"year\": 2025,\n        \"access_level\": \"private\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnother document:\n\n```\n{\n    \"text\": \"Python programming tutorial...\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n        \"department\": \"Engineering\",\n        \"year\": 2026,\n        \"access_level\": \"public\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNow the user asks:\n\n> Find Python documentation from Engineering.\n\nYou can apply:\n\n```py\nfilter = {    \n  \"department\": \"Engineering\",    \n  \"access_level\": \"public\"\n}\n```\n\nThe database performs vector retrieval with the relevant metadata constraints.\n\n**Why is this important?**\n\nWithout filtering, the database might return:\n\n- Documents from unrelated departments.\n- Outdated information.\n- Documents the user shouldn't access.\n- Irrelevant categories.\n\nMetadata filtering is therefore important for both relevance and security. It applied before vector similarity search.\n\nProduction warning: Metadata filters are not a replacement for authorization. Your application must enforce access permissions using trusted user identity and server-side policy. Never trust a user-provided filter as the only access-control mechanism.\n\n# Hybrid Search\n\nImagine a user searches:\n\n> What is the error code ERR-502 in FastAPI?\n\nThe exact error code matters.\n\nA semantic search system might retrieve documents discussing HTTP errors generally but fail to prioritize the exact string.\n\nThis is where hybrid search becomes useful.\n\nHybrid search combines:\n\n1. Sparse or keyword search.\n2. Dense vector search.\n\n## Sparse vector search\n\nSparse vectors contain mostly zero values, with only selected dimensions having nonzero values.\n\nA traditional example is a bag-of-words or TF IDF representation.\n\nSparse retrieval can be particularly useful for exact terms, technical identifiers, and rare keywords.\n\nA popular keyword based retrieval algorithm is BM25.\n\n## Dense vector search\n\nDense vectors are embeddings containing many numerical dimensions, typically with most dimensions nonzero.\n\nThey are useful for semantic similarity.\n\n## How hybrid search works\n\n![Hybrid Search Pipeline](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/hybrid-search-pipeline.png)\n\nOne common fusion technique is Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n\nIts formula is:\n\n$$ RRF(d)=\\sum{r\\in R} \\frac{1}{k+rank_r(d)} $$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $d$ = document.\n- $R$ = different ranked result lists.\n- $rank_r(d)$ = document rank in a result list.\n- $k$ = a smoothing constant.\n\nThe system combines the rankings from different retrieval methods.\n\nWhen should you use hybrid search?\n\n- Technical documentation.\n- Legal documents.\n- Product catalogs.\n- Code search.\n- Enterprise knowledge bases.\n- RAG applications containing exact IDs or technical terminology.\n\n# Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN)\n\nApproximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) is one of the most important concepts in vector databases, semantic search, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), recommendation systems, and modern AI applications.\n\nFirst, let's understand the problem ANN tries to solve.\n\nImagine you have 1 million documents. You convert every document into a vector using an embedding model.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Document   | Vector             |\n| ---------- | ------------------ |\n| Document A | [0.12, 0.45, 0.87] |\n| Document B | [0.15, 0.42, 0.81] |\n| Document C | [0.90, 0.10, 0.25] |\n| Document D | [0.11, 0.47, 0.85] |\n\nNow a user asks:\n\n\"What is machine learning?\"\n\nYou convert the query into an embedding vector:\n\n```\nquery_vector = [0.13, 0.44, 0.86]\n```\n\nYou want to retrieve documents whose vectors are closest to the query vector.\n\nThis is called Nearest Neighbor Search (NNS).\n\nThe objective is to find the vectors with the smallest distance from the query vector.\n\n![Vector Indexes](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/vector_indexes_ann.png)\n\nFor example:\n\n- Document A: Distance = 0.03\n- Document B: Distance = 0.08\n- Document C: Distance = 1.02\n- Document D: Distance = 0.02\n\nThe nearest document is D.\n\nThe second nearest document is A.\n\nThe third nearest document is B.\n\nThis is called Top-K Nearest Neighbor Search.\n\nMathematically:\n\nGiven a dataset:\n\n$$X=\\\\{x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_N\\\\}$$\n\nAnd a query vector $q$, find the K vectors with the smallest distance:\n\n$$TopK(q)=\\operatorname{arg\\\\,min}\\_{x_i\\in X}^{K}d(q,x_i)$$\n\nHere:\n\n- $X$ = collection of vectors.\n- $q$ = query vector.\n- $K$ = number of results.\n- $d$ = distance function.\n\n## Exact Nearest Neighbor Search\n\nBefore understanding ANN, you need to understand exact nearest neighbor search.\n\nSuppose you have 1 million vectors.\n\nWhen a query arrives, the system:\n\n1. Takes the query vector.\n2. Calculates its distance from the first vector.\n3. Calculates its distance from the second vector.\n4. Continues until it has calculated the distance from all 1 million vectors.\n5. Sorts the distances.\n6. Returns the closest K vectors.\n\nThis is called Brute-Force Search or Flat Search.\n\n![Bruteforce Search](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/brute_force_search_with_l2.png)\n\n```py\nimport numpy as np\n\nvectors = np.array([\n    [0.12, 0.45, 0.87],\n    [0.15, 0.42, 0.81],\n    [0.90, 0.10, 0.25],\n    [0.11, 0.47, 0.85]\n])\n\nquery = np.array([0.13, 0.44, 0.86])\n\n# Calculate Euclidean distance\ndistances = np.linalg.norm(vectors - query, axis=1)\n\n# Get nearest 3 vectors\nnearest_indices = np.argsort(distances)[:3]\n\nprint(nearest_indices)\nprint(distances[nearest_indices])\n```\n\nThis compares the query with every vector.\n\n### Complexity\n\nFor N vectors, each with D dimensions:\n\n$$Time Complexity = O(ND)$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- Number of vectors: 1,000,000\n- Dimensions: 1,536\n\nThe system performs approximately 1.536 billion coordinate-level distance calculations.\n\nThis becomes expensive when:\n\n- You have millions or billions of vectors.\n- You receive thousands of queries per second.\n- Your vectors have hundreds or thousands of dimensions.\n- You need low-latency responses.\n\nThis is where ANN becomes useful.\n\n## Approximate Nearest Neighbor\n\nApproximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) is a search technique that finds vectors that are very close to the query vector without necessarily finding the mathematically exact nearest vectors.\n\nInstead of comparing the query against every vector, ANN uses specialized indexing structures and search algorithms to reduce the number of comparisons.\n\n![Exact vs ANN](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/exact_vs_ann.png)\n\nConsider a dataset with 10 million vectors.\n\n### Exact search\n\n```\nQuery\n  |\n  v\nCompare with vector 1\nCompare with vector 2\nCompare with vector 3\n...\nCompare with vector 10,000,000\n  |\n  v\nReturn exact nearest neighbors\n```\n\n### Approximate search\n\n```\nQuery\n  |\n  v\nSearch an index\n  |\n  v\nExplore promising regions\n  |\n  v\nCompare only selected candidates\n  |\n  v\nReturn approximate nearest neighbors\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n| Search type  | Vectors examined | Result                         |\n| ------------ | ---------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| Exact search | 10,000,000       | Exact top 10                   |\n| ANN search   | 5,000            | Potentially approximate top 10 |\n\nThe numbers are illustrative, not guaranteed performance figures.\n\n**The important trade-off is:**\n\nANN sacrifices some search accuracy to achieve faster retrieval and lower computational cost.\n\nHowever, ANN doesn't mean random search or bad results. Well-designed ANN algorithms can achieve extremely high recall while searching a tiny fraction of the dataset.\n\n## Why Do We Need ANN\n\nLet's connect this to an actual AI application.\n\nImagine you're building a RAG application.\n\nYour application has:\n\n- 10 million documents.\n- An embedding model.\n- A vector database.\n- An LLM.\n\nA user asks:\n\n> What are the advantages of using microservices?\n\nYour application converts the question into an embedding.\n\nThen it needs to find the most relevant documents.\n\nWithout ANN:\n\n```\nUser Query\n    |\n    v\nEmbedding Model\n    |\n    v\nQuery Vector\n    |\n    v\nCompare against 10 million vectors\n    |\n    v\nRetrieve Top 5 Documents\n    |\n    v\nLLM\n    |\n    v\nFinal Answer\n```\n\nWith ANN:\n\n```\nUser Query\n    |\n    v\nEmbedding Model\n    |\n    v\nQuery Vector\n    |\n    v\nANN Index\n    |\n    v\nExplore Relevant Candidates\n    |\n    v\nRetrieve Top 5 Documents\n    |\n    v\nLLM\n    |\n    v\nFinal Answer\n```\n\nANN makes it possible to perform semantic retrieval efficiently at large scale.\n\nOther applications include:\n\n- Image similarity search.\n- Face and object recognition systems.\n- Recommendation engines.\n- Product search.\n- Document retrieval.\n- Audio similarity search.\n- Duplicate detection.\n- Multimodal search.\n\n## How Does ANN Work Internally\n\nThis is the most important part to understand.\n\nANN generally works through two major stages:\n\n1. Index construction (indexing phase)\n2. Query searching (search phase)\n\nThe idea is to organize vectors into a structure that allows the system to avoid examining the entire dataset.\n\n### Index construction\n\nSuppose you have 1 million vectors.\n\nInstead of storing them as an unorganized collection, an ANN algorithm builds an index.\n\nDepending on the algorithm, the index might contain:\n\n- A graph connecting similar vectors.\n- Clusters grouping similar vectors.\n- Hash tables grouping vectors into buckets.\n- Compressed vector representations.\n\nFor example, imagine these 12 vectors:\n\n![IVF Index Search](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/ivf_index_search.png)\n\n\nAn indexing algorithm might divide them into three clusters:\n\n```\nCluster A:\n  Vector 1\n  Vector 2\n  Vector 3\n  Vector 4\n\nCluster B:\n  Vector 5\n  Vector 6\n  Vector 7\n  Vector 8\n\nCluster C:\n  Vector 9\n  Vector 10\n  Vector 11\n  Vector 12\n```\n\nNow, when a query arrives, the system doesn't necessarily search all three clusters.\n\nIt identifies the most promising cluster or clusters and searches those.\n\nThis is one way ANN reduces search complexity.\n\nDifferent ANN algorithms organize the vectors differently, so let's explore them.\n\n### Query searching\n\nAfter constructing the index:\n\n1. Receive the query vector.\n2. Locate promising regions in the index.\n3. Retrieve candidate vectors.\n4. Calculate their actual distances or similarities.\n5. Rank the candidates.\n6. Return the Top-K results.\n\nAn important distinction:\n\nThe index helps identify candidates. The final candidate-ranking step usually calculates the actual distance using the selected distance metric.\n\nNot every algorithm follows exactly the same procedure, but this is the general idea.\n\n# Vector Indexing Algorithms\n\nThis is where you move from basic vector database knowledge to advanced engineering knowledge.\n\nThere are several indexing algorithms, but these are especially important:\n\n1. HNSW\n2. IVF\n3. PQ\n4. IVF-PQ\n5. DiskANN\n6. Flat (brute-force index)\n\n## HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World)\n\nHNSW is a graph-based Approximate Nearest Neighbor algorithm.\n\nIt organizes vectors into a multi-layer graph.\n\nEach vector is represented as a node.\n\nSimilar or nearby vectors are connected through edges.\n\n![HNSW](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/hnsw.png)\n\n### How does HNSW work?\n\nImagine you have 100,000 vectors.\n\nHNSW builds a graph with multiple layers.\n\n- Upper layers contain fewer nodes and provide long-distance navigation.\n- Lower layers contain more nodes and provide detailed local search.\n- The bottom layer contains all indexed nodes.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nLevel 2:\n       A -------- D\n        \\        /\n         \\      /\n\nLevel 1:\n   A ---- B ---- D\n    \\     |     /\n     \\    |    /\n\nLevel 0:\n A -- B -- C -- D -- E -- F -- G\n```\n\nThis is a conceptual illustration, not the exact structure of a generated HNSW index.\n\n**Search process**\n\nSuppose you want to find the nearest vector to query Q.\n\n1. Start from an entry point in the top layer.\n1. Compare Q with neighboring nodes.\n1. Move to a neighbor that is closer to Q.\n1. Continue until no better neighbor is found at that layer.\n1. Move down to the next layer.\n1. Search more deeply.\n1. At the bottom layer, explore a wider set of candidates.\n1. Return the top K nearest vectors.\n\n**Important HNSW parameters**\n\n| Parameter      | Meaning                                                                   |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `M`              | Maximum graph connections per node (with implementation-specific details) |\n| `efConstruction` | Search breadth during index construction                                  |\n| `efSearch`       | Search breadth during retrieval                                           |\n\nLet's understand them.\n\n**`M`**\n\nControls how many connections a node can have.\n\nHigher `M` generally means:\n\n- More graph connections.\n- More memory usage.\n- Potentially better recall.\n- More expensive index construction.\n\n**`efConstruction`**\n\n    - Controls how broadly the algorithm searches when building the graph.\n    - Higher values can improve graph quality, but increase indexing time.\n\n**`efSearch`**\n\n    - Controls the search breadth at query time.\n    - Higher `efSearch` generally improves recall but increases query latency.\n\n### Example configuration\n\n```\nindex_params = {    \n    \"M\": 16,    \n    \"efConstruction\": 200\n    }\n    \nsearch_params = {    \n        \"ef\": 100\n    }\n```\n\nThese are illustrative settings; actual parameter names and defaults depend on the vector database.\n\n**When should you use HNSW?**\n\n- Semantic search.\n- RAG.\n- Medium-to-large datasets.\n- Low-latency applications.\n- Frequently searched datasets.\n\nIts main disadvantage is memory consumption, especially for very large indexes.\n\n## IVF (Inverted File Index)\n\nIVF stands for Inverted File Index.\n\nIt divides the vector space into multiple clusters.\n\nIt generally uses a clustering algorithm such as K-means to identify cluster centroids.\n\nImagine you have 1 million vectors.\n\nInstead of searching through all vectors, IVF groups them into 1,000 clusters.\n\nEach cluster has a centroid.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nCluster 1: Technology vectors\nCluster 2: Food vectors\nCluster 3: Sports vectors\nCluster 4: Education vectors\n...\nCluster 1000\n```\n\nThese labels are just illustrative; actual clusters are determined by the vector geometry, not predefined semantic categories.\n\n## How IVF works\n\nIndex creation:\n\n1. Collect the vectors.\n2. Run a clustering algorithm such as K-means.\n3. Calculate cluster centroids.\n4. Assign each vector to its nearest centroid.\n5. Store the vectors in their corresponding inverted lists.\n\nSearch:\n\nSuppose the query is:\n\n\"How do I build a neural network?\"\n\n1. Convert the query into an embedding.\n2. Find the closest cluster centroids.\n3. Select a certain number of clusters.\n4. Search only the vectors inside those clusters.\n5. Return the top K results.\n\n### `nprobe`\n\n`nprobe` determines how many clusters are searched during retrieval.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nsearch_params = {    \"nprobe\": 10}\n```\n\nThis means the search examines 10 selected clusters.\n\nA larger nprobe:\n\n- Usually improves recall.\n- Increases search work.\n- Can increase latency.\n\nA smaller nprobe:\n\n- Usually reduces latency.\n- May miss relevant vectors.\n\n### When should you use IVF?\n\n- Large datasets.\n- Memory-conscious systems.\n- Datasets where partitioned search is useful.\n- Workloads where you can tune recall against latency.\n\nOne limitation is that if the relevant vector is in a cluster that wasn't selected, it won't be found.\n\n## PQ (Product Quantization)\n\nPQ stands for Product Quantization.\n\nIt is a vector compression technique.\n\nWhy do we need it?\n\nSuppose you have 100 million vectors.\n\nEach vector has 1,536 dimensions.\n\nIf each dimension uses a 32-bit float:\n\n$$ 1536 \\times 4 = 6144 \\text{ bytes} $$\n\nFor 100 million vectors:\n\n$$ 6144 \\times 100,000,000 $$\n\nThat is approximately 614.4 GB just for the raw vector values, excluding metadata and index overhead.\n\nThis is expensive.\n\nPQ reduces memory usage by compressing vectors.\n\n### How does PQ work\n\nSuppose you have this vector:\n\n$$ V=[0.2,0.5,0.8,0.1,0.9,0.4,0.3,0.7] $$\n\nInstead of treating it as one large vector, divide it into smaller subvectors.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$ V_1=[0.2,0.5] $$\n\n$$ V_2=[0.8,0.1] $$\n\n$$ V_3=[0.9,0.4] $$\n\n$$ V_4=[0.3,0.7] $$\n\nPQ learns codebooks for these subspaces.\n\nEach subvector is represented by the ID of its closest codeword.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nOriginal vector:\n[0.2, 0.5, 0.8, 0.1, 0.9, 0.4, 0.3, 0.7]\n\nCompressed representation:\n[12, 45, 8, 19]\n```\n\nThese IDs are illustrative.\n\nInstead of storing all original floating-point values, the system stores compact codes and shared codebooks.\n\n### Benefits of PQ\n\n- Significantly reduces memory consumption.\n- Can make large-scale search more economical.\n- Enables efficient approximate distance calculations.\n\n### Disadvantages\n\n- Compression introduces approximation error.\n- Recall can decrease.\n- Index training and tuning are required.\n\n## IVF-PQ\n\nIVF-PQ combines two techniques:\n\n- IVF: narrows the search to selected clusters.\n- PQ: compresses vectors for memory-efficient comparison.\n\nThe workflow:\n\n```\nInput vectors\n      |\n      v\nIVF clustering\n      |\n      v\nSelect candidate clusters\n      |\n      v\nPQ compression\n      |\n      v\nStore compact vector codes\n      |\n      v\nQuery-time approximate distance\n      |\n      v\nTop K results\n```\n\nThis is particularly useful for large-scale datasets where memory cost matters.\n\n## DiskANN\n\nDiskANN is an ANN approach designed to support very large vector indexes using SSD storage.\n\nThe motivation is straightforward:\n\nKeeping every vector and graph structure in RAM can become too expensive.\n\nDiskANN uses an optimized graph-based search design that can access index data from SSD while using RAM for important search structures and caching.\n\n### When is DiskANN useful?\n\n- Very large vector collections.\n- Memory-constrained environments.\n- SSD-backed indexes.\n- Production retrieval systems requiring economical scaling.\n\n## Flat index\n\nFlat indexing is essentially brute-force search.\n\nIt compares the query vector against every vector.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- Exact nearest-neighbor results.\n- Simple.\n- No approximate index-building process.\n- Useful as a benchmark for ANN recall.\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n- Expensive at large scale.\n- Search cost grows with the number of vectors.\n\n## Algorithm comparison\n\n| Algorithm | Main technique           | Main advantage                | Main limitation             |\n| --------- | ------------------------ | ----------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| Flat      | Brute force              | Exact results                 | Expensive at scale          |\n| HNSW      | Graph                    | Fast, high recall             | Memory intensive            |\n| IVF       | Clustering               | Reduced search space          | Can miss other clusters     |\n| PQ        | Compression              | Low memory                    | Approximation error         |\n| IVF-PQ    | Clustering + compression | Scalable and memory-efficient | More tuning                 |\n| DiskANN   | SSD-backed graph search  | Very large indexes            | More complex storage/search |\n\n## Production Uses\n\nThere is no single algorithm used by every production vector database.\n\nSome common examples:\n\n- FAISS supports Flat, IVF, PQ, and HNSW-related indexes.\n- Milvus supports multiple index types, including HNSW, IVF variants, and DiskANN in supported configurations.\n- Qdrant supports HNSW-based indexing and quantization.\n- Weaviate supports HNSW and other configurable index features.\n- Elasticsearch supports approximate kNN indexing, including HNSW-based approaches.\n\nThe correct choice depends on vector count, dimensions, RAM, latency requirements, update frequency, and recall requirements.\n\n\n# ChromaDB\n\nChromaDB (Chroma) is an open-source vector database designed to store, manage, and search vector embeddings. It is widely used in AI applications such as:\n\n- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\n- Semantic search\n- AI chatbots\n- Document question-answering systems\n- Recommendation systems\n- AI agents\n- Knowledge bases\n\nSince you're learning vector databases, FAISS, Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN), and semantic search, understanding ChromaDB will help you connect the theory with practical AI engineering.\n\nWe will learn it step by step, including Python code, internal mechanisms, architecture, and interview questions.\n\nChromaDB is a vector database that stores embeddings alongside associated documents and metadata, allowing applications to retrieve semantically similar information efficiently.\n\n## ChromaDB Architecture\n\n![ChromaDB Architecture](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/chromadb_architecture.png)\n\nChromaDB primarily works with these concepts:\n\n| Component    | Purpose                               |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------- |\n| Client       | Connects your application to ChromaDB |\n| Collection   | Groups related records                |\n| ID           | Unique identifier for each record     |\n| Embedding    | Numerical representation of content   |\n| Document     | Original text or content              |\n| Metadata     | Additional information about a record |\n| Vector index | Enables efficient similarity search   |\n| Query        | Searches for similar vectors          |\n\n\n### Client\n\nThe client is how your Python application communicates with ChromaDB.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"chromadbclient = chromadb.Client()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.98389Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.985002Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.023454Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:35.984956Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.0224Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":43},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"This creates an in-memory ChromaDB client.\n\nFor persistent local storage:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"chromadbclient = chromadb.PersistentClient(path=\"./chroma_db\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.024705Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.02505Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.083913Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.025021Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.082941Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":44},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The database data is stored locally in the `./chroma_db` directory and persists between application runs.\n\n### Collection\n\nA collection is similar to a table in a relational database.\n\nFor example:\n\n- `company_documents`\n- `product_catalog`\n- `research_papers`\n- `customer_support`\n\nCreate a collection:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection = chromadbclient.create_collection(name=\"company_documents\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.085192Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.085479Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.116674Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.085452Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.115529Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":45},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"You can also retrieve an existing collection:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection = client.get_collection(    name=\"company_documents\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.117836Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.118219Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.1399Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.118187Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.138849Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":46},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Or get it if it exists, otherwise create it:","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T15:43:01.994581Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T15:43:01.99487Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.043359Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T15:43:01.994845Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.041453Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection = client.get_or_create_collection(    name=\"company_documents\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.14124Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.141637Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.161922Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.141597Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.160905Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":47},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Create collection with HNSW configuration\ncollection = client.get_or_create_collection(\n    name=\"ann_demo\",\n    metadata={\n        \"hnsw:space\": \"cosine\",\n        \"hnsw:M\": 16,\n        \"hnsw:construction_ef\": 100,\n        \"hnsw:search_ef\": 50\n    }\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"A collection organizes your vector records.\n\n### IDs\n\nEvery record has a unique ID.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"ids = [    \"doc_001\",    \"doc_002\",    \"doc_003\"]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.163108Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.163419Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.185261Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.163389Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.184055Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":48},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"These IDs help you:\n\n- Update documents\n- Delete documents\n- Retrieve individual records\n- Prevent duplicate identifiers\n\n### Documents\n\nDocuments are the original text associated with an embedding.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"documents = [    \"ChromaDB is a vector database.\",    \"FastAPI is a Python web framework.\",    \"LangGraph is used to build stateful AI workflows.\"]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.186672Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.187123Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.207125Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.187085Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.205955Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":49},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"ChromaDB can store these documents together with their embeddings.\n\n### Metadata\n\nMetadata is additional structured information.\n","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.054682Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.055093Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.054929Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.054949Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"metadatas = [    \n    {        \n        \"category\": \"database\",        \n        \"source\": \"document1.pdf\",        \n        \"page\": 1    \n    },    {        \n        \"category\": \"backend\",    \n        \"source\": \"document2.pdf\", \n        \"page\": 4  \n    },    {      \n        \"category\": \"AI\",  \n        \"source\": \"document3.pdf\",     \n        \"page\": 2 \n    }\n]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.208335Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.20872Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.23772Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.208682Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.236697Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":50},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Metadata allows you to filter search results.\n\nFor example:\n\n> Search only documents belonging to the AI category.\n\nThis is extremely useful in real-world RAG applications.\n\n## How ChromaDB generates embeddings\n\nThere are two main approaches.\n\n### Approach A: ChromaDB generates embeddings automatically\n\nChromaDB can use an embedding function internally.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection.add(    \n    ids=[\"1\", \"2\"],  \n    documents=[        \n        \"I love artificial intelligence\",   \n        \"Machine learning is interesting\"   \n    ]\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.238808Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.239182Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.000718Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:36.239149Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:38.999831Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"/root/.cache/chroma/onnx_models/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/onnx.tar.gz: 100%|██████████| 79.3M/79.3M [00:00<00:00, 93.5MiB/s]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":51},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"You don't need to manually provide vectors here.\n\nChromaDB uses its configured embedding function to convert the documents into embeddings.\n\nThe default embedding function in the documented setup uses the `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` model through ONNX Runtime.&#x20;\n\nWhen you query:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"results = collection.query(    \n    query_texts=[\"What is AI?\"],    \n    n_results=2\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.002025Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.002394Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.372187Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.002364Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.370846Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":52},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"ChromaDB embeds the query and searches for similar vectors.\n\n### Approach B: You generate embeddings yourself\n\nThis is especially useful when you want to use:\n\n- OpenAI embedding models\n- Google Gemini embedding models\n- Hugging Face embedding models\n- Your own custom embedding model\n\nWhen you use your embedding, you are responsible for generating the query embedding with the appropriate model.\n\nWhen you use ChromaDB's embedding, ChromaDB handles embedding generation using its configured embedding function.\n\n## ChromaDB CRUD operations\n\nCRUD means:\n\n- Create\n- Read\n- Update\n- Delete\n\n### Create / Add","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection.add(\n    ids=[\"101\", \"102\"],\n    documents=[\n        \"Python is a programming language.\",\n        \"FastAPI is used to build APIs.\"\n    ],\n    metadatas=[\n        {\"category\": \"programming\"},\n        {\"category\": \"backend\"}\n    ]\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.373135Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.37345Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.850851Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.373421Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.849969Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":53},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Read / Get\n\nGet records using their IDs:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"results = collection.get(\n    ids=[\"101\", \"102\"]\n)\n\nprint(results)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.851804Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.852145Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.858781Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.852113Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.857718Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"{'ids': ['101', '102'], 'embeddings': None, 'documents': ['Python is a programming language.', 'FastAPI is used to build APIs.'], 'uris': None, 'included': ['metadatas', 'documents'], 'data': None, 'metadatas': [{'category': 'programming'}, {'category': 'backend'}]}\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":54},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Read by metadata filter:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"results = collection.get(\n    where={\"category\": \"backend\"}\n)\n\nprint(results[\"documents\"])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.86007Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.860374Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.88546Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.860346Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.884554Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"['FastAPI is used to build APIs.']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":55},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Update\n\nSuppose a document has changed.\n","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.082439Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.083518Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.082927Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.082952Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection.update(\n    ids=[\"101\"],\n    documents=[\n        \"Python is a popular programming language used in AI.\"\n    ],\n    metadatas=[\n        {\"category\": \"AI programming\"}\n    ]\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.886655Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.887068Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.28275Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:39.887028Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.281344Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":56},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"ChromaDB updates the record and its embedding based on the new document content when an embedding function is configured.\n\n### Upsert\n\n`upsert` means insert or update.","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.092327Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.093309Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.092877Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T15:43:02.092924Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection.upsert(\n    ids=[\"101\", \"103\"],\n    documents=[\n        \"Updated Python information.\",\n        \"New document about neural networks.\"\n    ]\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.283997Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.284327Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.851435Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.284295Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.850318Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":57},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nIf an ID already exists, its record is updated. If it doesn't exist, a new record is inserted.\n\n### Delete\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection.delete(    \n    ids=[\"102\"]\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.852602Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.852984Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.896433Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.852943Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.8953Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":58,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"{'deleted': 1}"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":58},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Delete records using a filter:\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"collection.delete(    \n    where={\"category\": \"backend\"}\n)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.8977Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.898074Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.921182Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.898044Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.920168Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":59,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"{'deleted': 0}"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":59},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"These operations are fundamental when building a production RAG system because the knowledge base changes over time.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Metadata filtering in ChromaDB\n\nSemantic search alone is not enough for real-world applications.\n\nImagine you have a product catalog with:\n\n- 10,000 products\n- Product categories\n- Prices\n- Availability\n- Brands\n\nUser query:\n\n> \"Find affordable laptops under $800.\"\n\nYou need both semantic search and structured filtering.\n\n### Metadata filtering\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"results = collection.query(\n    query_texts=[\"Affordable laptop for programming\"],\n    n_results=5,\n    where={\n        \"$and\": [\n            {\"category\": \"laptop\"},\n            {\"price\": {\"$lt\": 800}},\n            {\"available\": True}\n        ]\n    }\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.922429Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.922826Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.299497Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:40.922784Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.298423Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":60},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nThis example shows a combination of conditions.\n\nThe result is restricted to records that match the metadata conditions, then ranked by semantic similarity.\n\nChroma supports metadata filtering through `where` and document-content filtering through `where_document`.&#x20;\n\n**Common metadata operators**\n\n| Operator | Meaning                        |\n| -------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `$eq`    | Equals                         |\n| `$ne`    | Not equal                      |\n| `$gt`    | Greater than                   |\n| `$gte`   | Greater than or equal          |\n| `$lt`    | Less than                      |\n| `$lte`   | Less than or equal             |\n| `$in`    | Value is in a list             |\n| `$nin`   | Value is not in a list         |\n| `$and`   | All conditions must match      |\n| `$or`    | At least one condition matches |\n\nFor example:\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"results = collection.query(\n    query_texts=[\"AI engineering courses\"],\n    n_results=10,\n    where={\n        \"$and\": [\n            {\"category\": {\"$eq\": \"education\"}},\n            {\"year\": {\"$gte\": 2024}}\n        ]\n    }\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.300848Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.301592Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.67023Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.30155Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.669051Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":61},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Document filtering\n\nSuppose you want documents containing the word \"Python\".\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"results = collection.get(\n    where_document={\n        \"$contains\": \"Python\"\n    }\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.671389Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.671722Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.682085Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.671692Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T16:24:41.680716Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":62},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"This is different from semantic search.\n\nSemantic search retrieves by meaning.\n\nDocument filtering searches for content matching a condition.\n\n# Advanced Production Concepts\n\nKnowing how to insert and retrieve vectors is not enough to build a production-grade AI system.\n\nYou should understand these additional concepts.\n\n## Vector dimensionality\n\nDimensionality means the number of values in an embedding.\n\nDifferent embedding models produce different dimensions.\n\n| Model                                   | Dimensions |\n| --------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| all-MiniLM-L6-v2                        | 384        |\n| BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5                   | 768        |\n| OpenAI text-embedding-3-small (default) | 1536       |\n| OpenAI text-embedding-3-large (default) | 3072       |\n\nWhy does dimensionality matter?\n\n- Higher dimensions generally require more storage.\n- Higher dimensions can increase computational costs.\n- Different models produce different embedding spaces.\n- Your database collection must have the correct dimension.\n\nImportant: You cannot simply insert a 768-dimensional vector into a collection configured for 384 dimensions.\n\nAlso, higher dimensionality does not automatically mean better retrieval quality.\n\n## Vector normalization\n\nNormalization means scaling a vector to a particular magnitude, often 1.\n\nFormula:\n\n$$ A\\_{normalized}=\\frac{A}{||A||} $$\n\nExample:\n\n$$ A=[3,4] $$\n\nMagnitude:\n\n$$ ||A||=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5 $$\n\nNormalized vector:\n\n$$ A'=[0.6,0.8] $$\n\nWhy normalize?\n\n- Removes magnitude differences.\n- Makes dot product equivalent to cosine similarity for unit vectors.\n- Can provide consistent retrieval behavior when the model expects normalized embeddings.\n\nHowever, always follow your embedding model's recommendations.\n\n## Metadata indexing\n\nMetadata filtering can become expensive when you have millions of records.\n\nVector databases may support indexes for metadata fields.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\n{\n    \"category\": \"AI\",\n    \"department\": \"Engineering\",\n    \"year\": 2026\n}\n```\n\nYou might create indexes on:\n\n- category\n- department\n- year\n\nThis can improve filtering performance, depending on the database and query pattern.\n\n## Quantization\n\nQuantization reduces the precision used to represent vector values.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nOriginal:\nfloat32\n\nCompressed:\nint8 or lower-bit representation\n```\n\nIt reduces memory requirements and can improve search efficiency.\n\nHowever, compression can introduce numerical error and affect retrieval quality.\n\nRemember the difference:\n\n- PQ is a specific vector compression technique based on codebooks and subvectors.\n- Scalar quantization maps individual numerical values to a smaller representation.\n- Binary quantization represents dimensions using binary values.\n\n## Updating and deleting vectors\n\nA production application needs to support:\n\n- Insert.\n- Update.\n- Delete.\n- Upsert.\n\nFor example, a company updates its privacy policy.\n\nYour RAG system must update the old document embeddings.\n\nA good ingestion pipeline should:\n\n1. Identify the changed source document.\n2. Remove or replace its old chunks.\n3. Generate new chunks.\n4. Generate new embeddings.\n5. Insert the updated vectors.\n6. Verify the updated records.\n\nUse stable document IDs and chunk IDs to prevent duplicate or stale records.","metadata":{}}]}